name: undefined behaviour
intro: Undefined behaviour (UB) is where the C standard stops promising.
intro: Not "unspecified result" -- no promise at all about the whole program.
intro: The compiler is allowed to assume it never happens, and it optimises
intro: on that assumption. So UB is worse than a crash: it can look fine in
intro: testing and break in production, or break only at `-O2`.
intro:
intro: The catalogue you will meet:
intro:
intro:     signed overflow       INT_MAX + 1, and abs(INT_MIN), -INT_MIN
intro:     shifting too far      x << 32 on a 32-bit int, or a negative count
intro:     out of bounds         a[10] on a[10]; a pointer more than
intro:                           one past the end
intro:     uninitialised read    int x; use(x);
intro:     use after free        read or write after free(p); free(p) twice
intro:     NULL dereference      *p where p is NULL
intro:     strict aliasing       reading a float's bytes through an int *
intro:     unsequenced writes    i = i++ + 1;  f(i++, i++)
intro:
intro: Why the optimiser makes it worse: `if (x + 1 < x)` is meant to catch
intro: overflow, but since overflow cannot happen the compiler deletes the
intro: test. A loop `for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)` may be assumed to end, even
intro: when n is INT_MAX: signed overflow is UB, so the compiler assumes
intro: `i` never wraps. Code after `*p` is assumed to run only with p not
intro: NULL, so a NULL check placed after the dereference may vanish.
intro:
intro: The fix is to never get there. Check the operands first:
intro:
intro:     if (b > 0 && a > INT_MAX - b)  /* a + b would overflow */
intro:
intro: Unsigned arithmetic wraps by definition (mod 2^N), so it is safe but
intro: silently wrong if you wanted a real sum. `__builtin_add_overflow` is a
intro: GCC and clang extension; it is not standard C and tcc lacks it, so
intro: for portable code write the range check yourself.
intro:
intro: Habits that catch the rest: initialise everything, pass a length with
intro: every buffer and check it, set pointers to NULL after free, and use
intro: `assert(cond)` for things that must be true (it is compiled out with
intro: NDEBUG, so never put a side effect inside it). Compile with
intro: `-Wall -Wextra`; build tests with `-fsanitize=undefined,address`
intro: (gcc/clang only).
---
q: strint.c -- turn text into an int, every edge handled
spec: For each command-line argument, in order, print one line saying what
spec: it is as a decimal `int`:
spec:
spec:   `empty`      the argument is the empty string
spec:   `bad`        it begins with whitespace, or is not a whole number
spec:                (no digits, trailing characters, `0x10`, `1.5`, `-`)
spec:   `range`      it is a number but does not fit in an int
spec:   `ok N`       the value, printed in decimal
spec:
spec: An optional leading `+` or `-` is allowed. No arguments prints
spec: nothing. Exit 0.
file: strint.c
prog: #include <ctype.h>
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <limits.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
prog: 		const char *s = argv[i];
prog: 		if (s[0] == '\0') {
prog: 			puts("empty");
prog: 			continue;
prog: 		}
prog: 		if (isspace((unsigned char)s[0])) {
prog: 			puts("bad");
prog: 			continue;
prog: 		}
prog: 		char *end;
prog: 		errno = 0;
prog: 		long v = strtol(s, &end, 10);
prog: 		if (end == s || *end != '\0')
prog: 			puts("bad");
prog: 		else if (errno == ERANGE || v < INT_MIN || v > INT_MAX)
prog: 			puts("range");
prog: 		else
prog: 			printf("ok %ld\n", v);
prog: 	}
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: strint 42 -7 +5 0 -0
case: strint '' ' 5' 5x - + 0x10 1.5 2>&1
case: strint 2147483647 2147483648 -2147483648 -2147483649 99999999999999999999 -99999999999999999999
case: strint
hint: strtol with errno zeroed first; check endptr, the empty string, leading space (strtol skips it), then ERANGE or a value outside INT_MIN and INT_MAX
